**What the code does** (`internal/proxy/shardclient/refined_look_aside_balancer.go:256-266`):

```go
if math.Float64frombits(metrics.serviceTimeSmooth.Load()) == 0 {
    // seed with the fresh sample
} else {
    // EWMA
}
```

It treats a smoothed service time of exactly 0 as meaning "not yet seeded". When that is true, it overwrites both the service-time and response-time smoothed values with the raw sample, with no damping.

**What it is meant to do** (`docs/design-docs/design_docs/20261019-look-aside-balancer-fix-design.md`, "Design Details → C", the `UpdateCostMetrics` snippet at lines 135-146 and the "Seeding" bullet at line 160):

```go
if nowTs-metrics.ts.Load() > b.metricExpireInterval {
    // seed with the fresh sample
} else {
    // EWMA
}
```

The seed happens only when the previous sample has expired (`now - ts > metricExpireInterval`) or never existed (`ts == 0`). The document explains why `0` must not be the marker. A smoothed ServiceTime of 0 is a normal value for sub-millisecond requests, because the wire metric is truncated to integer milliseconds. The document names two failures this would cause:

1. After a run of 0ms service-time samples, one 20ms outlier skips the EWMA. The smoothed value jumps straight to 20 instead of moving to about 4. This defeats fix C, which exists to damp outliers.
2. A long run of 0s can underflow the float back to 0, which triggers re-seeding again.

